6. DOM
DOM = Document Object Model
The DOM allows JavaScript to access and change HTML elements.
Consider this HTML:
<h1 id="title">Hello World</h1>

<button id="btn">Change Text</button>
JavaScript can access the <h1>:
const title = document.getElementById("title");

console.log(title);

getElementById()
const title = document.getElementById("title");

title.textContent = "Welcome to JavaScript";
The HTML changes from:
Hello World
to:
Welcome to JavaScript

querySelector()
const title = document.querySelector("#title");
You can also select a class:
const card = document.querySelector(".card");

querySelectorAll()
Selects multiple elements.
HTML:
<p class="text">Hello</p>
<p class="text">Welcome</p>
<p class="text">JavaScript</p>
JavaScript:
const paragraphs = document.querySelectorAll(".text");

console.log(paragraphs);

Changing CSS
HTML:
<h1 id="title">Hello</h1>
JavaScript:
const title = document.getElementById("title");

title.style.color = "blue";
title.style.fontSize = "40px";

Changing HTML
const box = document.getElementById("box");

box.innerHTML = "<h2>Welcome</h2>";
textContent vs innerHTML
element.textContent
Changes text.
element.innerHTML
Can insert HTML.

7. Events
Events are actions performed by the user or browser.
Examples:
Click
Mouse movement
Keyboard input
Form submission
Page loading

click Event
HTML:
<button id="btn">Click Me</button>
JavaScript:
const button = document.getElementById("btn");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    alert("Button clicked!");
});

Using Arrow Function
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
    alert("Button clicked!");
});

Example: Change Text on Button Click
HTML
<h1 id="title">Hello World</h1>

<button id="btn">Change Text</button>
JavaScript
const title = document.getElementById("title");
const button = document.getElementById("btn");

button.addEventListener("click", () => {

    title.textContent = "Welcome to TechSolutions";

});

Example: Change Background Color
HTML
<div id="box">
    Hello
</div>

<button id="btn">
    Change Color
</button>
JavaScript
const box = document.getElementById("box");
const button = document.getElementById("btn");

button.addEventListener("click", () => {

    box.style.backgroundColor = "blue";
    box.style.color = "white";

});

Example: Form Event
HTML
<form id="myForm">

    <input
        type="text"
        id="name"
        placeholder="Enter Name"
    >

    <button type="submit">
        Submit
    </button>

</form>
JavaScript
const form = document.getElementById("myForm");

form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {

    event.preventDefault();

    const name = document.getElementById("name").value;

    console.log(`Welcome ${name}`);

});
event.preventDefault() prevents the browser's default form submission.
